WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:01.557
Nine in the morning.

2
00:00:01.577 --> 00:00:03.780
Everyone opens a dashboard.

3
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.005
At the same time, analysts run heavy ad-hoc queries.

4
00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:08.415
They collide.

5
00:00:08.435 --> 00:00:12.465
Organize capacity by what the business needs first.

6
00:00:12.485 --> 00:00:13.590
Think of a hospital.

7
00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.575
Emergencies go first.

8
00:00:15.595 --> 00:00:18.075
An interactive query is the emergency.

9
00:00:18.095 --> 00:00:20.385
It runs as soon as possible.

10
00:00:20.405 --> 00:00:21.860
A batch query can wait.

11
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.295
It starts when resources are free.

12
00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:26.645
So send non-urgent reports as batch.

13
00:00:26.665 --> 00:00:29.420
They stop fighting the dashboards.

14
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.705
Next: who gets compute first?

15
00:00:31.725 --> 00:00:36.815
Give each kind of work its own reservation: dashboards, pipelines, ad-hoc.

16
00:00:36.835 --> 00:00:38.777
Set a baseline on the important ones.

17
00:00:38.797 --> 00:00:41.125
Autoscaling covers the bursts.

18
00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:43.650
And keep dev and test away from prod.

19
00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.435
An experiment must not starve production.

20
00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:50.085
To size it, watch slot use in the admin charts.

21
00:00:50.105 --> 00:00:51.345
Recap.

22
00:00:51.365 --> 00:00:52.720
Interactive runs now.

23
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:53.733
Batch waits.

24
00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:57.180
Reservations and baselines decide who goes first.

25
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.975
Try the checkpoint below.
